= 200 \\). By the pigeonhole principle, at least one pair must have an intersection of at least 200.\n6. **Constructing the Example**: A configuration where each pair of subsets intersects in exactly 200 elements is possible by appropriately distributing elements between subsets, confirming that the minimum maximum intersection is indeed 200.\n\nThus, the maximum positive integer \\( r \\) that satisfies the given condition is \\(\\boxed{200}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Upper Bound Proof**:\n    - We need to show that \\( r \\leq 200 \\).\n    - Let us define \\( k \\) from \\( \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 10\\} \\). We focus on subsets \\( A_k \\) defined as follows:\n    \\[\n    A_k = \\{100k - 99, 100k - 98, \\ldots, 100k\\}\n    \\]\n    - Consider these particular collections of these \\( A_k \\) sets:\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    &A_1 \\cup A_5 \\cup A_6 \\cup A_7 \\cup A_9, \\\\\n    &A_1 \\cup A_2 \\cup A_7 \\cup A_8 \\cup A_{10}, \\\\\n    &A_2 \\cup A_3 \\cup A_6 \\cup A_8 \\cup A_9, \\\\\n    &A_3 \\cup A_4 \\cup A_7 \\cup A_9 \\cup A_{10}, \\\\\n    &A_4 \\cup A_5 \\cup A_6 \\cup A_8 \\cup A_{10}\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\]\n    - Each combination above has 200 elements. Therefore, we see that the intersection count \\( r \\leq 200 \\).\n\n2. **Upper Bound Establishment**:\n    - We introduce \\( a_{ij} \\) such that:\n    \\[\n    a_{ij} = \n    \\begin{cases} \n    1, & \\text{if } i \\in A_j \\\\\n    0, & \\text{otherwise}\n    \\end{cases}\n    \\]\n    - Let \\( m_i \\) be the count of subsets \\( A_j \\) containing element \\( i \\); thus:\n    \\[\n    m_i = \\sum_{j=1}^{5} a_{ij}\n    \\]\n    - For \\( i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 1000 \\) and \\( j = 1, 2, \\ldots, 5 \\), the sum \\( \\sum_{i=1}^{1000} m_i = 2500 \\).\n\n3. **Calculate the Intersection Sum**:\n    - The double-counted sum of intersections between pairs of these 5 subsets is given by:\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq 5} |A_i \\cap A_j| = \\sum_{i=1}^{1000} \\binom{m_i}{2