radictions. Therefore, the only possible solution is when the previous assumption is invalid. Wait, but we know that there is at least one solution: a=b=c=1, where p=q=r=2. So, this suggests that the above analysis is missing something.\n\nWait, in the case when a=1, which is odd, but we have:\n\np =b^c +1. For p to be prime. If a=1 (odd), then to make p odd, b^c must be even. So b must be even, as before. Then, q =1^b +c=1 +c. Since q is prime, c must be such that c +1 is prime, so c=1 (q=2), c=2 (q=3), etc. Also, r =c^1 +b =c +b. Which must be prime.\n\nBut earlier, when a=1, c=1:\n\np=1^1 +1=2, q=1^1 +1=2, r=1^1 +1=2. All primes. But according to our earlier analysis, if a is odd, then b must be even. But here, a=1, which is odd, but b=1, which is odd. Contradicts the earlier conclusion that b must be even. So, where did the analysis go wrong?\n\nAh! When a is odd, we concluded that b must be even to make p =b^c + a odd (since a is odd, and p must be odd, hence b^c must be even). But in the case a=1, b=1 (odd), c=1, then b^c=1, which is odd. So p=1 +1=2, which is even and prime. So p=2. Therefore, in this case, even though a is odd, p is allowed to be 2 (the only even prime). Hence, our earlier analysis assumed that p is an odd prime, but p can be 2. So, we need to adjust our analysis to account for p, q, r possibly being 2.\n\nTherefore, going back to the parity analysis:\n\nIf a is odd, then p =b^c + a. For p to be prime, either:\n\n- p=2, which requires b^c +a=2. Since a\u22651 (odd), a=1. Then b^c=1. Hence, b=1, c any. So b=1, c=1. Therefore, p=1 +1=2.\n\nOr:\n\n- p is an odd prime. Then, since a is odd, b^c must be even (so b even). Thus, in this case, if p is odd, then b must be even. If p=2, then a=1 and b^c=1, which implies b=1, c=1.\n\nSimilarly, for other variables.\n\nSo, perhaps we need to split into cases where p, q, or r is 2.\n\nCase 1: At least one of p, q, r is 2.\n\nLet's suppose p=2. Then:\n\np =b^c +a=2. Since a \u22651, b^c +a \u22651 +1=2. So equality holds only when b^c=1 and a=1. Therefore, b=1 and c=1, since 1^c=1 for any c. Hence, a=1, b=1, c=1. Then q=1^1 +1=2 and r=1^1 +1=2. So all three are 2. Hence, equal.\n\nSimilarly, if q=2, then q=a^b +c=2. So a^b +c=2. Since a,b,c \u22651, a^b \u22651, so c=1. Then a^b=1 \u21d2 a=1, b any. But then c=1. So a=1, c=1, b any. Then p =b^1 +1 =b +1, which must be prime. r =1^1 +b=1 +b. So p=r. Hence, p and r are both equal to b +1. Therefore, p=r. Hence, two primes equal.\n\nSimilarly, if r=2, then r=c^a +b=2. So c^a +b=2. Since c,a \u22651, c^a \u22651, so b=1. Then c^a=1 \u21d2 c=1, a any. Then p =b^c +a=1 +a, which must be prime. q =a^b +c=a +1, which must be prime. Hence, p =a +1 and q =a +1. Therefore, p=q. So two primes equal.\n\nTherefore, in any case where one of p, q, r is 2, we get that at least two of them are equal.\n\nCase 2: All p, q, r are odd primes greater than 2.\n\nIn this case, none of p, q, r is 2, so they are all odd primes. Then, similar to the previous analysis:\n\nFrom p =b^c +a: since p is odd, b^c and a must be of opposite parity. If b is even, then b^c is even, so a must be odd. If b is odd, then b^c is odd, so a must be even.\n\nSimilarly, for q =a^b +c: since q is odd, a^b and c must be of opposite parity. If a is even, a^b is even, so c must be odd. If a is odd, a^b is odd, so c must be even.\n\nFor r =c^a +b: since r is odd, c^a and b must be of opposite parity. If c is even, c^a is even, so b must be odd. If c is odd, c^a is odd, so b must be even.\n\nTherefore, we have a system of parity conditions:\n\nFrom p: (b even \u2192 a odd) and (b odd \u2192 a even)\nFrom q: (a even \u2192 c odd) and (a odd \u2192 c even)\nFrom r: (c even \u2192 b odd) and (c odd \u2192 b even)\n\nLet's try to resolve these parity constraints.\n\nAssume a is even. Then:\n\nFrom q: a even \u2192 c odd\nFrom p: a even \u2192 since a is even, from p =b^c +a being odd, b^c must be odd. Hence, b must be odd.\nFrom r: c is odd \u2192 c odd, so from r: c odd \u2192 c^a is odd (since c is odd and a is any). Therefore, to make r odd, b must be even.\n\nBut from p: b is odd. Contradiction. So if a is even, then we must have b odd (from p) and b even (from r). Contradiction. Hence, a cannot be even.\n\nTherefore, a must be odd.\n\nIf a is odd:\n\nFrom q: a odd \u2192 c even\nFrom p: a odd \u2192 p odd requires b^c even \u2192 b even\nFrom r: c even \u2192 c^a even \u2192 r odd requires b odd\n\nBut from p: b even. From r: b odd. Contradiction. Hence, no solution when a is odd and all p, q, r are odd primes greater than 2.\n\nTherefore, the only possible solutions are those where at least one of p, q, r is 2. But as we saw in Case 1, when any of p, q, r is 2, it forces at least two of them to be equal. Hence, in all cases, at least two of p, q, r must be equal.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that at least two of \\( p, q, r \\) must be equal when \\( a, b, c \\) are natural numbers and \\( p = b^c + a \\), \\( q = a^b + c \\), \\( r = c^a + b \\) are primes, we analyze the parity constraints and possible cases:\n\n1. **Case 1: At least one of \\( p, q, r \\) is 2**:\n   - If \\( p = 2 \\), then \\( b^c + a = 2 \\). This implies \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b^c = 1 \\), so \\( b = 1 \\) and \\( c = 1 \\). Thus, \\( q = 1^1 + 1 = 2 \\) and \\( r = 1^1 + 1 = 2 \\), making all three equal.\n   - If \\( q = 2 \\), then \\( a^b + c = 2 \\). This implies \\( c = 1 \\) and \\( a = 1 \\). Then \\( p = b^1 + 1 = b + 1 \\) and \\( r = 1^1 + b = b + 1 \\), so \\( p = r \\).\n   - If \\( r = 2 \\), then \\( c^a + b = 2 \\). This implies \\( b = 1 \\) and \\( c = 1 \\). Then \\( p = 1^1 + a = a + 1 \\) and \\( q = a^1 + 1 = a + 1 \\), so \\( p = q \\).\n\n2. **Case 2: All \\( p, q, r \\) are odd primes**:\n   - Parity analysis leads to contradictions:\n     - If \\( a \\) is even, then \\( b \\) must be odd (from \\( p \\)) and even (from \\( r \\)), which is impossible.\n     - If \\( a \\) is odd, then \\( c \\) must be even (from \\( q \\)) and \\( b \\) must be even (from \\( p \\)) and odd (from \\( r \\)), another contradiction.\n\nSince all scenarios where \\( p, q, r \\) are odd primes lead to contradictions, the only valid solutions require at least one of \\( p, q, r \\) to be 2, forcing at least two primes to be equal. Hence, in all cases, **at least two of \\( p, q, r \\) must be equal**.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{At least two of \\( p, q, \\) and \\( r \\) must be equal.}", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Let's consider three natural numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\). We know:\n   \\[\n   p = b^{c} + a, \\quad q = a^{b} + c, \\quad r = c^{a} + b\n   \\]\n   and \\(p\\), \\(q\\), \\(r\\) are prime numbers. We need to prove that at least two of \\(p\\), \\(q\\), and \\(r\\) are equal.\n\n2. Firstly, observe that among the three natural numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), there must be at least two numbers with the same parity (both odd or both even), by the pigeonhole principle. Assume without loss of generality that \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) have the same parity.\n\n3. Notice that:\n   \n   - If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are both even, then \\(p = b^{c} + a\\) will be even (since \\(b^{c}\\) and \\(a\\) are both even).\n   - If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are both odd, then \\(b^{c}\\) will be odd (since an odd number raised to any power remains odd), \\(a\\) will also be odd, thus \\(p = b^{c} + a\\) will be even.\n\n4. Since \\(p\\) is a prime number and the only even prime number is 2, we must have:\n   \\[\n   p = 2\n   \\]\n\n5. From the equation \\(p = b^c + a\\), we deduce:\n   \\[\n   b^c + a = 2\n   \\]\n\n6. Since \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are natural numbers, the possible pairs \\((b, a)\\) that satisfy this equation are:\n   \\[\n   1^c + a = 2 \\implies 1 + a = 2 \\implies a = 1, \\quad c \\ge 1\n   \\]\n\n7. Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n      & a = 1, \\\\\n      & b = 1 \n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n8. Now, calculate \\(q\\) and \\(r\\):\n   \\[\n   q = a^b + c = 1^1 + c = 1 + c = c + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   r = c^a + b = c^1 + 1 = c + 1\n   \\]\n\n9. Therefore:\n    \\[\n    q = r = c + 1\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nWe have shown that \\( q \\) and \\( r \\) are indeed equal. Hence, the given conditions ensure that at least two among \\( p \\), \\( q \\), and \\( r \\) are equal primes. Therefore, we conclude:\n\\[\n\\boxed{q = r}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Out of the first ten thousand numbers, how many end with the digit 1 and can be expressed in the form \\(8^m + 5^n\\) where \\(m \\in \\mathbb{N}\\) and \\(n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to figure out how many numbers up to 10,000 end with the digit 1 and can be written in the form \\(8^m + 5^n\\) where m and n are natural numbers. Let me start by breaking down the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, let's clarify the parameters. The numbers in question must satisfy two conditions:\n1. They are of the form \\(8^m + 5^n\\).\n2. They end with the digit 1 (i.e., their last digit is 1).\n3. They are within the first ten thousand numbers, which I assume means numbers from 1 up to 10,000 inclusive.\n\nAlso, since m and n are natural numbers, they start from 1. So m \u2265 1 and n \u2265 1.\n\nTo approach this, maybe I should first consider the possible values of \\(8^m\\) and \\(5^n\\) modulo 10 because we're interested in the last digit of their sum. If the last digit is 1, then \\((8^m + 5^n) \\mod 10 = 1\\).\n\nLet me start by analyzing the last digits of \\(8^m\\) and \\(5^n\\).\n\nStarting with \\(8^m\\):\n\n- \\(8^1 = 8\\) \u2192 last digit 8\n- \\(8^2 = 64\\) \u2192 last digit 4\n- \\(8^3 = 512\\) \u2192 last digit 2\n- \\(8^4 = 4096\\) \u2192 last digit 6\n- \\(8^5 = 32768\\) \u2192 last digit 8\n- ... and then the cycle repeats every 4: 8, 4, 2, 6, 8, 4, 2, 6, etc.\n\nSo the last digit of \\(8^m\\) cycles every 4 with the pattern [8, 4, 2, 6].\n\nNow for \\(5^n\\):\n\n- \\(5^1 = 5\\) \u2192 last digit 5\n- \\(5^2 = 25\\) \u2192 last digit 5\n- ... and this continues. All powers of 5 end with 5.\n\nSo the last digit of \\(5^n\\) is always 5, regardless of n.\n\nTherefore, the sum \\(8^m + 5^n\\) modulo 10 is equal to (last digit of \\(8^m\\)) + 5 mod 10. Since we need this sum to be congruent to 1 mod 10, we have:\n\n(last digit of \\(8^m\\) + 5) \u2261 1 mod 10 \u21d2 (last digit of \\(8^m\\)) \u2261 (1 - 5) mod 10 \u21d2 (last digit of \\(8^m\\)) \u2261 -4 mod 10 \u21d2 6 mod 10.\n\nSo the last digit of \\(8^m\\) must be 6. Looking back at the cycle of \\(8^m\\)'s last digits [8, 4, 2, 6], the last digit is 6 when m \u2261 0 mod 4. But since m starts at 1, this corresponds to m = 4, 8, 12, 16, etc. So m must be a multiple of 4.\n\nTherefore, for \\(8^m\\) to end with 6, m must be 4k where k is a natural number (k \u2265 1). So m can be 4, 8, 12, 16, etc.\n\nNow, given that m must be such that \\(8^m\\) is part of the sum \\(8^m + 5^n\\) and the total sum must be \u226410,000, we can find the possible values of m and n.\n\nFirst, let's find the maximum possible m such that \\(8^m \u2264 9995\\) (since \\(5^n\\) is at least 5, so \\(8^m \u2264 10,000 - 5 = 9995\\)).\n\nCalculating \\(8^m\\):\n\n- \\(8^1 = 8\\)\n- \\(8^2 = 64\\)\n- \\(8^3 = 512\\)\n- \\(8^4 = 4096\\)\n- \\(8^5 = 32768\\)\n\nWait, \\(8^5 = 32768\\) which is way more than 9995. So the maximum m where \\(8^m \u2264 9995\\) is m=4 (4096). Then m=5 would be 32768, which is too big. So possible m values are 1,2,3,4. But earlier, we saw that m must be a multiple of 4 for the last digit of \\(8^m\\) to be 6. So among m=1,2,3,4, only m=4 satisfies that. Therefore, m must be 4. Wait, but m can be 4, 8, 12, etc., but \\(8^8\\) is 16777216, which is way larger than 10,000. So actually, m can only be 4 in this case. Because higher multiples of 4 (like 8,12,...) would make \\(8^m\\) exceed 10,000 even before adding \\(5^n\\). Hence, m=4 is the only possible value where \\(8^m\\) ends with 6 and \\(8^m + 5^n \u2264 10,000\\).\n\nTherefore, the only possible m is 4. Let me check that again. If m=4, \\(8^4 = 4096\\). Then \\(5^n\\) must be such that 4096 + \\(5^n\\) \u2264 10,000. Therefore, \\(5^n\\) \u2264 5904. Let's find the maximum n such that \\(5^n \u2264 5904\\).\n\nCalculating powers of 5:\n\n- \\(5^1 = 5\\)\n- \\(5^2 = 25\\)\n- \\(5^3 = 125\\)\n- \\(5^4 = 625\\)\n- \\(5^5 = 3125\\)\n- \\(5^6 = 15625\\)\n\n15625 is larger than 5904, so the maximum n is 5. So n can be from 1 to 5. So for m=4, n can be 1,2,3,4,5.\n\nTherefore, the possible numbers are:\n\n- \\(8^4 + 5^1 = 4096 + 5 = 4101\\)\n- \\(8^4 + 5^2 = 4096 + 25 = 4121\\)\n- \\(8^4 + 5^3 = 4096 + 125 = 4221\\)\n- \\(8^4 + 5^4 = 4096 + 625 = 4721\\)\n- \\(8^4 + 5^5 = 4096 + 3125 = 7221\\)\n\nWait, but 7221 is less than 10,000, so that's okay. Let's check the last digit of each of these:\n\n- 4101 ends with 1\n- 4121 ends with 1\n- 4221 ends with 1\n- 4721 ends with 1\n- 7221 ends with 1\n\nYes, all these numbers end with 1. So that's five numbers so far. But wait, is m=4 the only possible m? Let me check if there are other m values where \\(8^m\\) ends with 6 and \\(8^m\\) is small enough such that adding \\(5^n\\) gives a total \u226410,000.\n\nWait, the next m where the last digit is 6 would be m=8. Let's compute \\(8^8\\). \\(8^8 = (8^4)^2 = 4096^2 = 16,777,216\\). That's way over 10,000, so adding any \\(5^n\\) (even 5) would make it larger than 10,000. Therefore, m=8 is too big. Similarly, m=12 is even larger. So indeed, only m=4 is valid.\n\nTherefore, the only possible numbers are the five generated by m=4 and n=1 to 5. So that's five numbers.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me confirm if there's another possibility where even if \\(8^m\\) doesn't end with 6, but when added to 5^n (which ends with 5), the total ends with 1. Wait, earlier logic said that since 5^n always ends with 5, then \\(8^m\\) must end with 6 because 6 + 5 = 11, which ends with 1. That makes sense. So the last digit of \\(8^m\\) must be 6. Therefore, only m=4,8,12,... but m=4 is the only one feasible.\n\nHence, only m=4, leading to five numbers as n ranges from 1 to 5.\n\nBut wait, maybe there's a mistake here. Let me check again. Suppose m=4k, but k=1 (m=4), gives \\(8^4 = 4096\\). If n=6, \\(5^6 = 15625\\), but 4096 + 15625 = 19721, which is over 10,000, so n can't be 6. So maximum n=5 as previously determined.\n\nTherefore, the numbers are 4101, 4121, 4221, 4721, 7221. Let's check if these are all \u226410,000. 7221 is less than 10,000, yes. 4721 is also less. So all five numbers are valid.\n\nBut wait, is there another m where \\(8^m\\) ends with 6, which is m=4,8,12,..., but m=8 gives \\(8^8 = 16,777,216\\), which is way over 10,000. So indeed, only m=4.\n\nTherefore, the total numbers are five. But let me confirm once more.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's a case where even if the last digit of \\(8^m\\) is not 6, but when added to 5, the carryover makes the last digit 1. Wait, but addition modulo 10 is straightforward. Let's double-check:\n\nIf \\(8^m \\mod 10 = d\\), then \\(d + 5 \\equiv 1 \\mod 10\\). So \\(d \u2261 -4 \\mod 10\\), which is 6. So the last digit must indeed be 6. Therefore, the earlier conclusion holds.\n\nTherefore, only m=4 is possible. So the numbers are those five. Hence, the answer should be 5.\n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check for m=0. Wait, but the problem states m is a natural number. Depending on the definition, natural numbers sometimes start at 1, sometimes at 0. But in the problem statement, it's specified m \u2208 \u2115 and n \u2208 \u2115. Depending on the convention, \u2115 sometimes starts at 1. But to be sure, let me check if m=0 is allowed. If m=0, then \\(8^0 = 1\\), so \\(1 + 5^n\\). Then, let's see if those numbers end with 1.\n\nSo \\(5^n\\) ends with 5, so 1 + 5 = 6, which ends with 6. So even if m=0, \\(8^0 + 5^n = 1 + 5^n\\), which would end with 6. So even if m=0 is allowed, which it's not per the problem statement (since m \u2208 \u2115, and \u2115 here is likely starting at 1). Therefore, m=0 is not considered.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion remains five numbers.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let's check for other possible m values where \\(8^m\\) might have a different last digit but when added to \\(5^n\\) somehow gets a last digit 1. Wait, according to the earlier analysis, since \\(5^n\\) always ends with 5, the last digit of \\(8^m\\) must be 6. Because 6 + 5 = 11, which ends with 1. There's no other digit that when added to 5 gives 1. Let's check:\n\nPossible last digits of \\(8^m\\) are 8,4,2,6. Adding 5 to each:\n\n- 8 + 5 = 13 \u2192 ends with 3\n- 4 + 5 = 9\n- 2 + 5 = 7\n- 6 + 5 = 11 \u2192 ends with 1\n\nSo only when the last digit of \\(8^m\\) is 6 does the sum end with 1. Therefore, no other possibilities. Hence, only m=4 is valid.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 5. But wait, let me check if there's another way where \\(8^m + 5^n\\) could end with 1 even if the last digits don't directly add up. For example, maybe with larger exponents, but since we already constrained m and n such that the total is \u226410,000, and m must be 4, perhaps there's no other way.\n\nWait, let's take an example. Suppose m=4, n=5: 4096 + 3125 = 7221, which ends with 1. What if m=4, n=6: 4096 + 15625 = 19721, which is over 10,000, so invalid. Similarly, m=4, n=0: but n must be \u22651. So n=1 to 5.\n\nAlternatively, could there be another m where \\(8^m\\) is less than 10,000 and ends with 6? For example, m=4: 4096, which ends with 6. Next is m=8, which is 16,777,216, way over. So no.\n\nTherefore, only m=4, n=1 to 5. So five numbers.\n\nBut wait, let me verify each number:\n\n1. 4096 + 5 = 4101 \u2714\ufe0f ends with 1\n2. 4096 + 25 = 4121 \u2714\ufe0f ends with 1\n3. 4096 + 125 = 4221 \u2714\ufe0f ends with 1\n4. 4096 + 625 = 4721 \u2714\ufe0f ends with 1\n5. 4096 + 3125 = 7221 \u2714\ufe0f ends with 1\n\nAll correct, and all are \u226410,000. So these are five numbers. Hence, the answer is 5.\n\nBut hold on. Wait, let me check if there are any other numbers where \\(8^m\\) ends with 6, but m is not 4. For example, m=4k where k=1,2,... but \\(8^4 = 4096\\), \\(8^8\\) is way too big. So no. Therefore, only m=4.\n\nHence, the total number is 5. Therefore, the answer should be \\boxed{5}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{5}", "deepseek_solution": "To determine how many numbers up to 10,000 end with the digit 1 and can be expressed in the form \\(8^m + 5^n\\) where \\(m \\in \\mathbb{N}\\) and \\(n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\), we analyze the last digits of \\(8^m\\) and \\(5^n\\):\n\n1. **Last digit analysis**:\n   - The last digit of \\(8^m\\) cycles every 4: 8, 4, 2, 6.\n   - The last digit of \\(5^n\\) is always 5.\n\n2. **Condition for last digit 1**:\n   - The sum \\(8^m + 5^n\\) must end in 1. This requires the last digit of \\(8^m\\) to be 6 (since \\(6 + 5 = 11\\)).\n\n3. **Possible values of \\(m\\)**:\n   - The last digit of \\(8^m\\) is 6 when \\(m \\equiv 0 \\mod 4\\). The valid \\(m\\) values within the range are 4 (since \\(8^8\\) exceeds 10,000).\n\n4. **Possible values of \\(n\\)**:\n   - For \\(m = 4\\), \\(8^4 = 4096\\). The maximum \\(n\\) such that \\(4096 + 5^n \\leq 10,000\\) is \\(n = 5\\) (since \\(5^5 = 3125\\)).\n\n5. **Valid numbers**:\n   - Calculating the sums for \\(m = 4\\) and \\(n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\):\n     - \\(4096 + 5 = 4101\\)\n     - \\(4096 + 25 = 4121\\)\n     - \\(4096 + 125 = 4221\\)\n     - \\(4096 + 625 = 4721\\)\n     - \\(4096 + 3125 = 7221\\)\n\nAll these numbers end with 1 and are within the range up to 10,000. Thus, there are 5 such numbers.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. We need to find the number of integers among the first ten thousand integers that end in 1 and can be represented in the form \\(8^{m} + 5^{n}\\) where \\(m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\).\n\n2. We start by identifying the necessary conditions for an integer to satisfy the given equation:\n    \\[ 8^m + 5^n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\]\n\n3. We examine the units digit of \\(8^m\\):\n    - \\(8^1 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{10}\\)\n    - \\(8^2 \\equiv 64 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{10}\\)\n    - \\(8^3 \\equiv 512 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{10}\\)\n    - \\(8^4 \\equiv 4096 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{10}\\)\n    - The units digit repeats every four powers: 8, 4, 2, 6.\n\n4. We examine the units digit of \\(5^n\\):\n    - \\(5^1 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{10}\\)\n    - \\(5^2 \\equiv 25 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{10}\\)\n    - \\(5^3 \\equiv 125 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{10}\\)\n    - \\(5^n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{10}\\) for any \\(n \\geq 1\\).\n\n5. Now, we determine the feasible combinations for \\(8^m + 5^n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10}\\):\n    - Since \\(5^n \\equiv 5 \\pmod{10}\\), we need:\n        \\[ 8^m + 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\rightarrow 8^m \\equiv -4 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{10} \\]\n\n6. This means that the units digit of \\(8^m\\) must be 6:\n    - From the cyclic pattern of \\(8^m\\), we see that \\(8^m\\) ends in 6 only if \\(m \\equiv 4 \\pmod{4}\\).\n\n7. Therefore, \\(m\\) must be a multiple of 4 for it to be \\(8^{m} + 5^{n}\\).\n\n8. Each number \\(8^{4k} + 5^n\\) where \\(k \\in \\mathbb{N}\\), and \\(8^{4k} \\equiv 6 \\pmod{10}\\) and plus \\(5\\) will be:\n    \\[ 8^{4k} + 5 \\equiv 6 + 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10} \\]\n    - This confirms \\(8^{4k} + 5^n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10}\\).\n\n9. We are interested in the first ten thousand integers (\\(1\\) to \\(10,000\\)), formatted for ending in 1:\n    - The numbers ending in 1 are \\(1, 11, 21, \\ldots, 9991\\).\n\n10. For a complete calculation, from:\n    - Numbers in an arithmetic sequence \\(1, 11, 21, \\ldots, 9991\\) :\n        - The common difference, \\(d = 10\\), Starting term \\(a_1 = 1\\):\n            \\[\n            a_{n} = 9991 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 10\n            \\]\n        - Solving for \\(n\\):\n            \\[\n            9991 = 1 + 10(n-1) \\rightarrow 9990 = 10(n-1) \\rightarrow 999 = n-1 \\rightarrow n = 1000\n            \\]\n\n11. Finally, there are 1000 such numbers among the first ten thousand integers \\( \\text{end in 1} \\). However, only:\n    \\boxed{5}\n", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "An engineering project was worked on by Person B alone for 4 days, then by Persons A and C together for 6 days, and finally, Person A completed the remaining work in 9 days. Knowing that the work completed by Person B is \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of the work completed by Person A, and the work completed by Person C is 2 times the work completed by Person B. Determine the time each of Persons A, B, and C would need to complete the project if working alone.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this engineering project problem. Hmm, so there are three people: A, B, and C. The project was done in three phases: first, Person B worked alone for 4 days. Then Persons A and C worked together for 6 days. Finally, Person A finished the remaining work in 9 days. \n\nWe also know that the amount of work Person B did is 1/3 of what Person A did in total. And the work done by Person C is twice that of Person B. We need to find how long each person would take to complete the project alone. \n\nLet me start by assigning variables. Let's say the total work is 1 project. Let\u2019s denote the work rates of A, B, and C as a, b, and c respectively. Work rate is typically defined as the fraction of the project completed per day. So, if Person A can finish the project in x days alone, their work rate is 1/x per day, right? Similarly for B and C.\n\nBut here, we have a situation where different people worked on different days. Let me break down the work done in each phase.\n\nFirst phase: Person B works alone for 4 days. So the work done by B is 4b.\n\nSecond phase: Persons A and C work together for 6 days. Their combined work rate is (a + c), so the work done in this phase is 6(a + c).\n\nThird phase: Person A works alone for 9 days. Work done here is 9a.\n\nTotal work is the sum of these three parts: 4b + 6(a + c) + 9a = 1.\n\nSimplify that: 4b + 6a + 6c + 9a = 1 => (6a + 9a) + 4b + 6c = 1 => 15a + 4b + 6c = 1. So that's equation one.\n\nNow, the other information given: work completed by B is 1/3 of the work completed by A. Let's parse this. The total work done by B is 4b (since B only worked in the first phase). The total work done by A is the work done in the second and third phases: 6a + 9a = 15a. So according to the problem, 4b = (1/3)(15a). Let me check that.\n\nYes: \"the work completed by Person B is 1/3 of the work completed by Person A\". So B's total work equals 1/3 of A's total work. So 4b = (1/3)(15a). Let's compute that: 15a divided by 3 is 5a. So 4b = 5a => b = (5/4)a. That's equation two.\n\nNext, the work completed by Person C is 2 times the work completed by Person B. So C's total work is 2 times B's. C only worked in the second phase for 6 days, so work done by C is 6c. B's total work is 4b. So 6c = 2*(4b) => 6c = 8b => c = (8/6)b = (4/3)b. That's equation three.\n\nNow we have three equations:\n\n1) 15a + 4b + 6c = 1\n\n2) b = (5/4)a\n\n3) c = (4/3)b\n\nLet me substitute equations 2 and 3 into equation 1. So first, substitute b in terms of a into equation 3.\n\nFrom equation 2: b = (5/4)a. Then equation 3 becomes c = (4/3)*(5/4)a = (5/3)a.\n\nSo now, c is (5/3)a.\n\nNow substitute b and c into equation 1:\n\n15a + 4*(5/4)a + 6*(5/3)a = 1.\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\nFirst term: 15a.\n\nSecond term: 4*(5/4)a = 5a.\n\nThird term: 6*(5/3)a = (6/3)*5a = 2*5a = 10a.\n\nSo adding them up: 15a + 5a + 10a = 30a.\n\nSo 30a = 1 => a = 1/30.\n\nSo Person A's work rate is 1/30 per day. That means Person A would take 30 days to complete the project alone.\n\nNow, find b from equation 2: b = (5/4)a = (5/4)*(1/30) = 5/120 = 1/24.\n\nSo Person B's work rate is 1/24 per day, meaning they would take 24 days alone.\n\nThen, c = (5/3)a = (5/3)*(1/30) = 5/90 = 1/18.\n\nPerson C's work rate is 1/18 per day, so they would take 18 days alone.\n\nWait, let me check these calculations again to make sure.\n\nFirst, a = 1/30: So A does 1/30 per day. Correct.\n\nThen b = 5/4 * a = 5/4 * 1/30 = 5/(120) = 1/24. Correct. So B does 1/24 per day, so 24 days.\n\nc = 4/3 * b = 4/3 * 1/24 = 4/72 = 1/18. Wait, but earlier I substituted c as (5/3)a. Wait, let's check:\n\nFrom equation 3: c = (4/3)b. Since b = 5/4 a, then c = (4/3)*(5/4 a) = (5/3)a. So c is (5/3)a. Since a is 1/30, c = (5/3)*(1/30) = 5/90 = 1/18. Yes, same result. So 1/18 per day, so 18 days for C.\n\nSo that seems consistent.\n\nBut let's verify all the initial conditions with these rates.\n\nFirst, total work done by B: 4 days * 1/24 per day = 4/24 = 1/6.\n\nTotal work done by A: 15a = 15*(1/30) = 15/30 = 1/2.\n\nCheck if B's work is 1/3 of A's work: 1/6 vs (1/3)*(1/2) = 1/6. Correct.\n\nTotal work done by C: 6 days * 1/18 per day = 6/18 = 1/3.\n\nCheck if C's work is 2 times B's work: 1/3 vs 2*(1/6) = 1/3. Correct.\n\nTotal work overall: 1/6 (B) + 1/3 (C) + 1/2 (A) = 1/6 + 2/6 + 3/6 = 6/6 = 1. Perfect.\n\nSo all conditions are satisfied. Therefore, the time each would take alone is:\n\nPerson A: 30 days,\n\nPerson B: 24 days,\n\nPerson C: 18 days.\n\nWait a second, hold on. But in the problem statement, the order of work is B for 4 days, then A and C together for 6 days, then A alone for 9 days. Let's just confirm that when we sum up the work done in each phase, it totals to 1.\n\nWork by B: 4 days * 1/24 = 1/6.\n\nWork by A and C together: 6 days. A's rate is 1/30, C's rate is 1/18. Combined, 1/30 + 1/18. Let's compute that:\n\n1/30 = 3/90,\n\n1/18 = 5/90,\n\nSo combined rate is 8/90 = 4/45 per day. Over 6 days, that's 6 * 4/45 = 24/45 = 8/15.\n\nThen work by A alone for 9 days: 9 * 1/30 = 9/30 = 3/10.\n\nTotal work: 1/6 + 8/15 + 3/10.\n\nConvert all to 30 denominator:\n\n1/6 = 5/30,\n\n8/15 = 16/30,\n\n3/10 = 9/30.\n\nAdding up: 5 + 16 + 9 = 30/30 = 1. Perfect. So that checks out.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A: 30 days, B:24 days, C:18 days.\n\n**Final Answer**\nPerson A would take \\boxed{30} days, Person B \\boxed{24} days, and Person C \\boxed{18} days to complete the project alone.", "deepseek_solution": "Given the engineering project worked on by Persons A, B, and C in three phases, we need to determine the time each person would take to complete the project alone. \n\n1. **Breakdown of Work Done:**\n   - Person B worked alone for 4 days.\n   - Persons A and C worked together for 6 days.\n   - Person A completed the remaining work in 9 days.\n\n2. **Define Work Rates:**\n   - Let the work rates of A, B, and C be \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) respectively (fraction of the project completed per day).\n\n3. **Total Work Equation:**\n   - Work done by B: \\(4b\\)\n   - Work done by A and C together: \\(6(a + c)\\)\n   - Work done by A alone: \\(9a\\)\n   - Total work: \\(4b + 6(a + c) + 9a = 1\\)\n   - Simplifying: \\(15a + 4b + 6c = 1\\)\n\n4. **Given Conditions:**\n   - Work completed by B is \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of the work completed by A: \\(4b = \\frac{1}{3}(15a) \\implies 4b = 5a \\implies b = \\frac{5}{4}a\\)\n   - Work completed by C is 2 times the work completed by B: \\(6c = 2(4b) \\implies 6c = 8b \\implies c = \\frac{4}{3}b\\)\n\n5. **Substitute \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) in terms of \\(a\\):**\n   - \\(b = \\frac{5}{4}a\\)\n   - \\(c = \\frac{4}{3}b = \\frac{4}{3} \\left(\\frac{5}{4}a\\right) = \\frac{5}{3}a\\)\n\n6. **Substitute into Total Work Equation:**\n   - \\(15a + 4\\left(\\frac{5}{4}a\\right) + 6\\left(\\frac{5}{3}a\\right) = 1\\)\n   - Simplify: \\(15a + 5a + 10a = 30a = 1 \\implies a = \\frac{1}{30}\\)\n\n7. **Calculate Work Rates:**\n   - \\(a = \\frac{1}{30}\\) (A completes the project in 30 days)\n   - \\(b = \\frac{5}{4}a = \\frac{5}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{30} = \\frac{1}{24}\\) (B completes the project in 24 days)\n   - \\(c = \\frac{5}{3}a = \\frac{5}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{30} = \\frac{1}{18}\\) (C completes the project in 18 days)\n\n8. **Verification:**\n   - Total work done by B: \\(4 \\times \\frac{1}{24} = \\frac{1}{6}\\)\n   - Total work done by A: \\(15 \\times \\frac{1}{30} = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\n   - Total work done by C: \\(6 \\times \\frac{1}{18} = \\frac{1}{3}\\)\n   - Check conditions: \\(\\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{3} = 2 \\times \\frac{1}{6}\\)\n   - Total work: \\(\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{2} = 1\\)\n\n### Final Answer\nPerson A would take \\boxed{30} days, Person B \\boxed{24} days, and Person C \\boxed{18} days to complete the project alone.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve this problem, we will let the time required for person \u7532, \u4e59, and \u4e19 to complete the job alone be \\( x \\) days, \\( y \\) days, and \\( z \\) days, respectively. We will use the information provided to build the equations needed to solve for \\( x \\), \\( y \\), and \\( z \\). \n\n1. **Determine the work rate for each person:**\n   - If \u7532 can complete the job in \\( x \\) days, his work rate is \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\) of the job per day.\n   - If \u4e59 can complete the job in \\( y \\) days, her work rate is \\( \\frac{1}{y} \\) of the job per day.\n   - If \u4e19 can complete the job in \\( z \\) days, his work rate is \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\) of the job per day.\n\n2. **Express the work done by each person including the given constraints.**\n\n   - \u4e59 works alone for 4 days, completing: \n     \\[\n     4 \\times \\frac{1}{y} = \\frac{4}{y} \\text{ of the job}\n     \\]\n\n   - \u7532 and \u4e59 work together for 6 days, completing: \n     \\[\n     6 \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y}\\right) = 6 \\left(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y}\\right) \\text{ of the job}\n     \\]\n\n   - The remaining work is completed by \u7532 alone in 9 days:\n     \\[\n     9 \\times \\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{9}{x} \\text{ of the job}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Combine the work done to form the equation for the whole job:**\n\n   Since \u4e59 alone works for 4 days, then \u7532 and \u4e59 work together for 6 days, and finally \u7532 finishes the job in 9 days, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4}{y} + 6 \\left(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y}\\right) + \\frac{9}{x} = 1 \n   \\]\n\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4}{y} + 6 \\left(\\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{y}\\right) + \\frac{9}{x} = 1 \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4}{y} + \\frac{6}{x} + \\frac{6}{y} + \\frac{9}{x} = 1 \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4 + 6}{y} + \\frac{6 + 9}{x} = 1 \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{y} + \\frac{15}{x} = 1 \n   \\]\n\n4. **Use the provided relationships between the work rates:**\n\n   - Given that \u516b completes \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\) of \u7532's work:\n     \\[\n     y = 3x \n     \\]\n   \n   - Given \u4e19 completes twice the workload of \u4e59:\n     \\[\n     z = \\frac{y}{2}\n     \\]\n     Simplifies to because \\(y = 3x\\):\n     \\[\n     z = \\frac{3x}{2}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Substitute \\( y = 3x \\) into the combined work equation:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{3x} + \\frac{15}{x} = 1 \n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{3x} + \\frac{45}{3x} = 1 \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{55}{3x} = 1 \n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   55 = 3x \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{55}{3}\n   \\]\n\n   However, we notice the problem states specific values that match with \u7532=30 (something might shift in the calculation or provided values should be confirmed once for our substituting values).\n  \nFrom the equation \\( x = 30 \\) goes with y from \u4ee3\u6570 relationships \\(y = 3 \\cdot 10 +  24 \\ because of x,\\ \\[\\zeta=18 and compares with correctness for all fractions values.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\\[\n\\text{The number of days required for each alone to complete the job are:}\n(\n\\boxed{30}, \n\\boxed{24}, \n\\boxed{18}\n)\nwbaps to absolute duty confirming.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find all three-digit numbers $\\overline{\u041c\u0413\u0423}$, comprised of different digits $M$, $\\Gamma$, and $Y$, for which the equality $\\overline{\\text{\u041c\u0413\u0423}} = (M + \\Gamma + Y) \\times (M + \\Gamma + Y - 2)$ holds.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let's tackle this problem. So, we need to find all three-digit numbers \u041c\u0413\u0423 where each digit M, \u0393, and Y are different, and the number itself equals (M + \u0393 + Y) multiplied by (M + \u0393 + Y - 2). Hmm, okay. Let's break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me make sure I understand the problem correctly. The three-digit number is formed by digits M, \u0393 (which is the Greek letter Gamma, but here it's just a digit, right?), and Y. All three digits must be distinct. The number, which is 100*M + 10*\u0393 + Y, should be equal to the product of (M + \u0393 + Y) and (M + \u0393 + Y - 2). So, mathematically, the equation is:\n\n100M + 10\u0393 + Y = (M + \u0393 + Y) \u00d7 (M + \u0393 + Y - 2)\n\nLet me denote S = M + \u0393 + Y for simplicity. Then the equation becomes:\n\n100M + 10\u0393 + Y = S \u00d7 (S - 2)\n\nSo, the problem reduces to finding all three-digit numbers with distinct digits where the number equals S(S - 2), where S is the sum of its digits.\n\nAlright, so first step: figure out possible values of S such that S(S - 2) is a three-digit number. Since the number is three-digit, S(S - 2) must be between 100 and 999 inclusive.\n\nSo, let's find the range of S that satisfies 100 \u2264 S(S - 2) \u2264 999.\n\nLet me solve the inequality S\u00b2 - 2S \u2265 100. Let's find S where this holds.\n\nS\u00b2 - 2S - 100 \u2265 0\n\nSolving the quadratic equation S\u00b2 - 2S - 100 = 0:\n\nDiscriminant D = 4 + 400 = 404\n\nSolutions: [2 \u00b1 \u221a404]/2 = [2 \u00b1 20.09975]/2\n\nPositive solution: (2 + 20.09975)/2 \u2248 11.05\n\nSo, S \u2265 11.05, so S \u2265 12.\n\nSimilarly, for the upper bound S(S - 2) \u2264 999:\n\nS\u00b2 - 2S - 999 \u2264 0\n\nSolve S\u00b2 - 2S - 999 = 0\n\nDiscriminant D = 4 + 3996 = 4000\n\nSolutions: [2 \u00b1 \u221a4000]/2 = [2 \u00b1 63.245]/2\n\nPositive solution: (2 + 63.245)/2 \u2248 32.622\n\nSo, S \u2264 32.622, hence S \u2264 32.\n\nTherefore, possible integer values of S are from 12 to 32 inclusive.\n\nBut wait, let me check for S=32:\n\n32*30 = 960, which is less than 999. But 32*30 = 960, so 32(S=32) would give 32*30=960. But 32*30=960. Wait, but S(S-2) is 32*30=960. So yes, 960 is a three-digit number. Similarly, for S=31: 31*29=899, which is still three-digit. S=12: 12*10=120, which is three-digit. So, indeed, S ranges from 12 to 32.\n\nWait, but wait: S=32 gives 32*30=960. So S can be up to 32, but let's confirm that 32*30=960. Then, S=33 would give 33*31=1023, which is four digits, so S must be at most 32.\n\nTherefore, S is in 12 to 32 inclusive.\n\nSo our possible S values are integers from 12 to 32. Now, for each S in this range, compute N = S(S - 2) and check if N is a three-digit number with distinct digits M, \u0393, Y where N = 100M + 10\u0393 + Y, and M \u2260 \u0393, \u0393 \u2260 Y, M \u2260 Y.\n\nSo, first, let's compute N for each S from 12 to 32, then check if N is a three-digit number with distinct digits.\n\nBut since we already know S ranges from 12 to 32, N will be in 12*10=120 to 32*30=960. Wait, but N is S(S - 2), so for S=12, 12*10=120; S=13, 13*11=143; up to S=32, 32*30=960. So all these N are three-digit numbers. Therefore, each S in 12-32 will produce a three-digit number. Now, for each such N, check if the digits M (hundreds), \u0393 (tens), Y (units) are all distinct.\n\nBut also, M cannot be zero because it's a three-digit number, so M is from 1-9, \u0393 and Y from 0-9, but all distinct.\n\nSo the plan is:\n\n1. For each S from 12 to 32:\n\n   a. Compute N = S*(S - 2)\n\n   b. Check if N is a three-digit number (it should be, since S ranges from 12 to 32)\n\n   c. Break N into digits: M (hundreds), \u0393 (tens), Y (units)\n\n   d. Check if M, \u0393, Y are all distinct\n\n   e. If yes, add N to the list of solutions\n\n2. After checking all S, output all such N.\n\nAlternatively, since S ranges fr