nce between the feet of the perpendiculars is independent of the choice of altitude and is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2R \\sin A \\sin B \\sin C}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R\\) is the circumradius of the triangle.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's denote the given acute-angled triangle as \\( \\triangle ABC \\), with \\( AD \\) being the altitude from vertex \\( A \\) to side \\( BC \\).\n\n1. Let \\( D \\) be the foot of the altitude \\( AD \\) from \\( A \\) to \\( BC \\).\n2. Draw perpendiculars from \\( D \\) to \\( AB \\) and \\( AC \\), and let these feet be \\( E \\) and \\( F \\) respectively.\n\nNext, observe the following properties and calculations involving these points:\n\n3. **Cyclic Quadrilateral**:\n    \\[\n    \\angle ADE = \\angle ADF = 90^\\circ\n    \\]\n    Since \\( AE \\perp DE \\) and \\( AF \\perp DF \\), quadrilateral \\( AEDF \\) forms a cyclic quadrilateral because opposite angles sum up to \\( 180^\\circ \\).\n\n4. **Circumcenter and Circumradius**:\n    Let \\( M \\) be the midpoint of \\( AD \\). \\( M \\) is the center of the circle circumscribing quadrilateral \\( AEDF \\) because in a right-angle cyclic quadrilateral, the midpoint of the hypotenuse is the center.\n   \n5. We must show that the distance \\( EF \\) is independent of the choice of altitude. Now let's find \\( EF \\).\n\n6. **Angle at the Center**:\n    Since \\( AEDF \\) is cyclic, angle \\( \\angle EMF \\) subtended by the arc \\( EF \\) at the center \\( M \\) is:\n    \\[\n    \\angle EMF = 2 \\angle A\n    \\]\n\n7. **Relating Sides and Angles**:\n    Using the property in a circle, \\( EF \\) can be given in terms of the radius \\( ME \\):\n    \\[\n    EF = 2 \\cdot ME \\cdot \\sin (\\angle EMF / 2) = 2 \\cdot ME \\sin (\\angle A)\n    \\]\n  \n8. **Calculate Radius \\( ME \\)**:\n    Notice \\( ME \\) is the median and can be expressed:\n    \\[\n    ME = \\frac{AD}{2}\n    \\]\n\n9. Therefore,:\n    \\[\n    EF = 2 \\cdot \\left( \\frac{AD}{2} \\right) \\cdot \\sin (\\angle A) = AD \\cdot \\sin (\\angle A)\n    \\]\n\n10. **Using Area to Express \\( AD \\)**:\n    Note that \\( AD = \\frac{2 \\cdot \\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC}{BC} \\), so we rewrite \\( EF \\):\n    \\[\n    EF = \\left( \\frac{2 \\times \\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC}{BC} \\right) \\cdot \\sin (\\angle A)\n    \\]\n    Applying the sine rule (\\(\\sin A = \\frac{a}{2R}\\) etc.):\n    \\[\n    EF = 2 \\left(\\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC}{BC}\\right) \\cdot \\sin (\\angle A)\n    \\]\n\n11. **General Representation**:\n    Similarly, if we choose altitudes from \\( B \\) and \\( C \\):\n    \\[\n    \\text{Distances from other altitudes are:}\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    2 \\left(\\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC}{AC}\\right) \\cdot \\sin (\\angle B)\n    \\]\n    \\[\n    2 \\left(\\frac{\\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC}{AB}\\right) \\cdot \\sin (\\angle C)\n    \\]\n\n12. **Equality By Sine Rule**:\n    Using the sine rule, and the property of areas:\n    \\[\n    \\text{All these distances simplify to the same value: } \\boxed{2 \\times \\text{Area of } \\triangle ABC \\times ( \\frac{\\text{sine terms}}{\\text{opposite sides}} )}\n    \\]\n    Which ultimately demonstrates they are equal and hence invariant to each altitude chosen:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Hence, the distance \\( EF \\) is independent of the altitude chosen.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "$a, b, c$ are three distinct natural numbers and $a + b + c = 48$. What is the maximum value of the product of $a, b,$ and $c$?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find three distinct natural numbers a, b, and c such that their sum is 48, and I want to maximize their product. Hmm, let's start by recalling some math concepts that might help here. I remember that for a given sum, the product of numbers is maximized when the numbers are as close to each other as possible. But wait, that's usually when the numbers don'