our triangles such that the sum of their areas is equal to the area of the parallelogram.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area}(PAB) + \\text{Area}(PBC) + \\text{Area}(PCD) + \\text{Area}(PDA) = S\n   \\]\n\n5. Recognize that each triangle's area can be calculated using the formula for the area of a triangle, $ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{base} \\cdot \\text{height}$, where the base is a segment of a side of the parallelogram, and the height is a segment drawn from point $P$ perpendicular to this base.\n\n6. Due to the properties of parallelograms and the way diagonals cut each other, the heights from $P$ to opposite sides are equal, and each pair of opposite triangles share the same height.\n   \n7. Let $h_1$ be the height from point $P$ to side $AB$ (this height is the same for $\\triangle PAB$ and $\\triangle PBC$ when projected onto $AD$ and $BC$ respectively).\n   \n8. Let $h_2$ be the height from point $P$ to the side $BC$ (this height is the same for $\\triangle PBC$ and $\\triangle PCD$ when projected onto $AB$ and $CD$ respectively).\n\n9. By considering the properties of the parallelogram and using symmetry, we can observe that:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area}(PAB) + \\text{Area}(PCD) = \\text{Area}(PDA) + \\text{Area}(PBC)\n   \\]\n\n10. To rigorously prove this, consider the two diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ which intersect at point $P$. These diagonals divide the parallelogram into four triangles whose total area equals the area of the parallelogram.\n\n11. The segments formed by these diagonals within the triangles share proportional parts of the areas due to the base-height relationship and the properties of similar triangles.\n\n12. Thus, by equating the sum of areas of triangles that share the same diagonals or sides, we can summarize:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area}(PAB) + \\text{Area}(PCD) = \\text{Area}(PBC) + \\text{Area}(PDA)\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Area}(PAB) + \\text{Area}(PCD) = \\text{Area}(PBC) + \\text{Area}(PDA)}\n\\]", "domain": "ma