ks brown. So this is invalid. Therefore, the colors C1, C2, C3 must all be different. So permutations must be all three colors.\n\nAnother permutation: C1=black, C2=brown, C3=white. Check:\n\nPosition 400 (1 mod 3): black \u2013 correct.\n\nPosition 2 (2 mod 3): brown \u2013 guessed white (error).\n\nPosition 20 (2 mod 3): brown \u2013 guessed brown (correct).\n\nPosition 800 (2 mod 3): brown \u2013 guessed white (error).\n\nPosition 600 (0 mod 3): white \u2013 guessed brown (error).\n\nTotal errors: positions 2,800,600 \u2013 three errors. Not acceptable.\n\nAnother permutation: C1=black, C2=white, C3=brown. This was our initial case, which gives one error at position 20.\n\nAnother permutation: C1=br, C2=bl, C3=w. Wait, let's try C1=brown, C2=black, C3=white. Then:\n\nPosition 400 (1 mod 3): brown \u2013 guessed black (error).\n\nPosition 2,20,800: black \u2013 guessed white, brown, white. So position 2: error (black vs white), 20: error (black vs brown), 800: error (black vs white). Position 600 (0 mod 3): white \u2013 guessed brown (error). Total errors: four. Too many.\n\nTherefore, the only valid permutation where there's only one error is when C1=black, C2=white, C3=brown. In this case, only position 20 is incorrect (guessed brown instead of white). \n\nBut wait, could there be another scenario where a different single guess is wrong? Let's consider if instead of position 20 being wrong, another position is wrong, but the colors still form a valid 3-periodic sequence.\n\nSuppose we assume that the error is in position 400. Let's see. So position 400 is guessed as black, but if it's actually, say, white. Then C1 would be white. Then let's see:\n\nIf C1=white (position 400 is white), then positions \u22611 mod 3 are white.\n\nC2 and C3 need to be determined. Let's look at other guesses:\n\nPosition 2 (2 mod 3): guessed white. If C2=white, then all positions 2 mod 3 are white. Then position 20 (2 mod 3) is guessed brown \u2013 error, position 800 (2 mod 3) guess